+# Hegel property-based testing report — gluon
+
+Property-based tests added with **hegel** (`hegeltest` crate) to the `gluon`
+crate. All tests live in `tests/stack_overflow.rs`, module `hegel_tests`
+(the existing robustness-focused test file — no new parallel test file was
+created, per the skill).
+
+Build/run: the crate only compiles its test targets with the `gluon_completion`
+feature enabled (a pre-existing `tests/vm.rs` completion test needs it), so tests
+run with:
+
+```
+cargo test --test stack_overflow --features gluon_completion hegel_tests
+```
+
+`.hegel/` was added to `.gitignore`.
+
+---
+
+## 1. Properties tested (+ evidence)
+
+Nine passing properties, one deterministically-pinned failing regression per real
+bug (two), and one `#[ignore]`d process-abort reproducer.
+
+| # | Property | Kind | Evidence |
+|---|----------|------|----------|
+| 1 | `parse_token_soup_never_panics` — parser returns Ok/Err (never panics) on source built from a vocabulary of gluon tokens (keywords, operators, punctuation, literals) | robustness / no-panic | Task's "compile/typecheck never panics on gluon-shaped token soup"; `parser::parse_expr` returns `Result` |
+| 2 | `parse_arbitrary_text_never_panics` — parser never panics on arbitrary `String`; tolerates *only* the two known pinned panics (asserts any other panic is a new bug) | robustness / no-panic | Parse-robustness catalogue pattern; this net *found both real bugs below* |
+| 3 | `parse_deep_parens_never_aborts` — parser survives nested `((((0))))` sweeping depth to 20 000 without aborting | recursion-depth robustness | Skill "probe recursion depth directly"; the highest-value class for interpreters/parsers |
+| 4 | `int_arithmetic_matches_checked_i64_oracle` — `#Int+ - * /` vs a Rust `i64` `checked_*` oracle (overflow / div-0 / `MIN`/`-1` → error) | differential oracle + boundary | `vm/src/thread.rs:2458` uses `VmInt::checked_{add,sub,mul,div}`; `binop_int` maps `None` → `Error::Message("Arithmetic overflow")` |
+| 5 | `evaluation_is_deterministic` — same source evaluated twice yields the same result | invariant | interpreter determinism |
+| 6 | `int_literal_roundtrips_through_eval` — `eval(n.to_string()) == n` for all `i64` incl. `MIN`/`MAX` | roundtrip + boundary | lexer `numeric_literal` (`parser/src/token.rs`); `VmInt = i64` |
+| 7 | `float_literal_roundtrips_through_eval` — `eval("{f:?}") == f` for finite `f64` in a no-exponent magnitude range | roundtrip | lexer float literal parses via Rust `str::parse::<f64>()`; gluon has **no exponent literal syntax** (verified: `1e30` fails to lex) |
+| 8 | `record_field_roundtrips_through_eval` — `{ a=.., b=.., c=.. }.field` returns the stored field | roundtrip / structural | task's "record field roundtrip"; verified `r.a` projection |
+| 9 | `string_literal_roundtrips_through_eval` — a string of safe (non-escape) codepoints round-trips through a gluon string literal | roundtrip | lexer `string_literal`; string eval verified |
+| P1 | `parse_invalid_string_escape_is_err` (**pinned failing**) — invalid escape must yield `Err`, not panic | no-panic contract | see bug 1 |
+| P2 | `parse_non_ascii_char_is_err` (**pinned failing**) — stray non-ASCII char must yield `Err`, not panic | no-panic contract | see bug 2 |
+| I1 | `typecheck_deep_nesting_stack_overflows` (**`#[ignore]`d**) — typecheck of deeply nested source aborts the process | recursion-depth abort | see bug 3 |
+
+**Liveness verified.** Every eval-based property (4–9) was mutation-checked: the
+core assertion was inverted and each test failed (counterexamples showed the
+assertion body executing, including for the guarded float/string properties, so
+none pass vacuously). The three no-panic parse properties funnel through the same
+`try_parse` helper that empirically panicked on the real bugs, and property 2
+caught both real panics before its known-bug tolerance was added.
+
+**Exploratory 10x run.** All nine passing properties were run at
+`test_cases = 1000` (single-threaded); all remained green with no new
+counterexamples, then reverted to the default count for commit.
+
+---
+
+## 2. Test results
+
+Default committed state (`--test-threads=1`):
+
+```
+9 passed; 2 failed; 1 ignored
+```
+
+The 2 failures are the deterministically-pinned real bugs (P1, P2); the 1 ignored
+test is the process-aborting stack overflow (I1). All other properties pass.
+
+---
+
+## 3. Suspected bugs
+
+### Bug 1 — Parser panics on invalid string escape (oracle-independent)
+
+- **Symptom:** parsing a string literal with an unrecognised escape (e.g. `"\q"`)
+  panics instead of returning a parse error.
+- **Panic:** `parser/src/token.rs:223` — `panic!("Invalid escape")` in
+  `unescape_string_literal`.
+- **Code path:** the lexer's `escape_code` (token.rs:519) *recovers* an unexpected
+  escape gracefully (`UnexpectedEscapeCode`), but the string token is stored as
+  `StringLiteral::Escaped(raw_slice)` and later re-scanned by
+  `unescape_string_literal`, whose `match` has a hard `_ => panic!("Invalid
+  escape")` arm. The two escape tables are inconsistent.
+- **Repro:** `parse_invalid_string_escape_is_err` (`try_parse(r#" "\q" "#)`).
+- **Severity:** a parser handling untrusted source must return `Err`, never panic.
+
+### Bug 2 — Parser panics on any non-ASCII character in an unexpected position (oracle-independent)
+
+- **Symptom:** any codepoint ≥ U+0080 in a position the grammar doesn't expect
+  (e.g. a bare `é`, `€`, or `"\u{80}"` as a whole expression) panics instead of
+  producing a parse error. ASCII controls such as `\u{7f}` (DEL) are handled
+  gracefully (return `Err`); the panic is specific to multi-byte UTF-8.
+- **Panic:** `parser/src/str_suffix.rs:83` —
+  `str::from_utf8(&buf).expect("UTF-8 string")` in `restore_char`
+  (`Utf8Error { valid_up_to: 0, error_len: Some(1) }`).
+- **Code path:** the "unexpected character" recovery arm of the tokenizer
+  (`token.rs:838`) calls `self.chars.chars.as_str_suffix().restore_char(&[ch])`
+  with a single lead byte; `restore_char` reassembles a 4-byte buffer and unwraps
+  `from_utf8`, which fails for the reconstructed lead byte and panics.
+- **Repro:** `parse_non_ascii_char_is_err` (`try_parse("é")`).
+- **Severity:** high — *any* source file containing a stray non-ASCII byte outside
+  a string/identifier crashes the front-end. Found by the arbitrary-text property.
+
+### Bug 3 — Typechecker/compiler stack overflow on deeply nested source (process abort)
+
+- **Symptom:** the parser alone handles nesting to depth 50 000 fine, but
+  `typecheck_str` (and thus `run_expr`) recurses per syntactic level and
+  **overflows the stack (SIGABRT)** at depth ≈ 1000. Reproduces with nested
+  parens `((((0))))`, operator chains `0 #Int+ 1 #Int+ …`, nested lambdas
+  `\x -> \x -> … 0`, and nested `let … in`.
+- **Code path:** post-parse tree walks (macro expansion / typecheck / compile) are
+  unbounded recursion over AST depth; there is no depth guard on untrusted source.
+- **Repro:** `#[ignore]`d `typecheck_deep_nesting_stack_overflows` (depth 5000).
+  Confirmed to abort with `signal: 6, SIGABRT` when run with `--ignored`.
+- **Why ignored:** the abort is not a catchable panic — an un-ignored failing test
+  would take the whole test binary down with it. Kept as a documented, ignored
+  reproducer per the skill's process-abort guidance. This is the classic
+  high-value untrusted-source stack-overflow class.
+- **Note:** gluon already has `dont_stack_overflow_on_let_bindings` (this file)
+  exercising ~360 shallow let bindings, showing the maintainers care about this
+  class — but the limit is not enforced at typecheck depth.
+
+---
+
+## 4. Unsound properties abandoned / adjusted
+
+- **Float roundtrip via `{:?}` with exponents.** An initial float roundtrip using
+  Rust's shortest-form `{:?}` fails for large/small magnitudes because gluon has
+  **no exponent literal syntax** (`1e30`, `1.23e30` fail to lex). This is an
+  unsupported-syntax input, not a value-corruption bug, so the property bounds the
+  magnitude to `[-1e15, 1e15]` and `tc.assume`s the formatted string contains no
+  `e`/`E` — keeping the roundtrip sound and exact (same `f64` parser on both ends).
+- **No property was abandoned outright.** The two escape/non-ASCII panics are kept
+  as pinned real bugs rather than being treated as generator over-breadth — a
+  parser must not panic on any input.
+- The broad `parse_arbitrary_text_never_panics` net does **not** suppress the two
+  known panics silently; it asserts a caught panic is one of the two known
+  messages and fails on anything else, so it keeps hunting for a third panic while
+  the two known ones are pinned by dedicated canonical tests.
+
+---
+
+## 5. Skill feedback
+
+- **The recursion-depth guidance paid off immediately.** Sweeping nesting depth
+  upward (rather than hoping a recursive generator wanders deep) found the
+  process-aborting typecheck stack overflow directly, and separately established
+  that the *parser* is robust to depth 50 000 — a useful "parser OK, later stage
+  not" split the skill's advice made easy to surface.
+- **The "arbitrary text no-panic" net over-delivered.** The plain
+  `generators::text()` property found bug 2 (non-ASCII panic) with a 1-character
+  counterexample — a reminder that the cheapest, broadest property is often the
+  most productive on parsers. Worth emphasising even more strongly that the
+  fully-arbitrary-text net should be written *first*, before fancier token-soup
+  generators.
+- **The known-bug tolerance pattern worked well** ("assert the caught panic is a
+  known one, else fail") to keep a broad no-panic net green and still hunting after
+  pinning specific bugs — matches the skill's "exclude known-buggy input from
+  sibling properties" advice; a short worked example of the *catch_unwind +
+  message match* form in the Rust reference would help.
+- **`catch_unwind` ergonomics:** the Rust reference could note that silencing the
+  panic hook around `catch_unwind` (and restoring it) avoids flooding stderr when a
+  no-panic property is expected to catch known panics frequently.
+- **Feature-flag build gotcha:** the workspace's default test build failed to
+  compile `tests/vm.rs` until `--features gluon_completion` was added. The skill's
+  "build the code under test" advice is fine, but a line about *reading CI scripts
+  to discover the required feature set* before assuming a build blocker would save
+  time (here `scripts/ci.sh` used `--all-features`).
